show_help_flatten() {
  cat <<EOF
my-git flatten  (alias: fl)

usage: $US [-V|-D] flatten [go] [-i] [--rm-git]

Merge every nested git repo under the CURRENT super-repo into the
super's history as regular tracked content. Must be run from the
repo's toplevel.

Modes:
  default (no 'go')   ANALYZE — print plan (list of nested .git dirs
                      and orphan gitlinks that WOULD be handled). No
                      writes of any kind.
  go                  APPLY — perform the flatten.

Preservation of nested histories (default):
  Each nested .git is briefly MOVED OUT of the outer worktree to a
  temp dir, 'git add -A && git commit' runs on the now-plain content,
  then each .git is MOVED BACK. Net effect:
    - outer's commit contains only the nested content (no gitlinks)
    - each nested repo's history stays intact ('cd nested && git log'
      still works)
    - future edits/adds/deletes inside nested dirs flow through the
      outer repo as normal tracked changes (git's embedded-repo
      detection only fires for NEW untracked dirs; after the first
      flatten commit, the nested paths are already tracked as content,
      so git treats the dir as a plain tracked directory that happens
      to contain a .git inside)
  A trap restores every moved .git on any error / SIGINT / SIGTERM.

--rm-git (destructive):
  Instead of move-out-and-restore, each nested .git is DELETED
  permanently. Requires typing literal 'yes' at a mandatory
  confirmation prompt listing every .git that will be deleted. No TTY
  = refusal. Only use when the nested history is genuinely disposable
  (e.g. a tree full of example / learning clones whose outer repo is
  where future work lives).

Why a dedicated operation:
  '.gitignore' with '.git/' or the full relative path does NOT flatten
  nested repos — git's embedded-repo check runs before gitignore rules
  and still stages the nested dir as a gitlink (mode 160000). There is
  no 'git add' flag that treats an embedded repo as content. Renaming
  or deleting the nested .git (what this subcommand automates) is the
  only mechanism that actually works.

Related:
  'my-git sm --unstage-embedded'  — ONLY unstages orphan gitlinks;
                                    doesn't touch the on-disk .git dirs.
                                    flatten does this as a first step.

Scope:
  Single-repo only (always). No -R / no walk into submodules. Must
  be run at the repo's toplevel (refuses from a subdir or outside any
  git repo).
EOF
}

show_help_sync() {
