my-git sync  (alias: syn)

usage: $US [-V|-D] sync [go] [-R|-i|--force] [paths...]

Composite "settle the tree" — runs sm → pull → mc in order. Each
phase receives the flags you passed to sync.

  Phase 1: sm    register new nested repos, rebind moved ones, clean
                 stale .gitmodules entries, .claude setup fixes.
  Phase 2: pull  fetch origin everywhere; fast-forward clean+behind.
  Phase 3: mc    auto-commit my-git-caused dirt (gitlink bumps from
                 phases 1+2, .gitignore/.claude fixes), then prompt-
                 gate user-dirt under -i; push where allowed.

Order rationale: sm first so registration is authoritative before
fetch; pull second so we pick up remote changes before committing
local; mc last so any gitlink bumps from earlier phases get captured
in the super's cleanup commit.

Options (all forwarded to each phase verbatim):
  go                 Actually act; otherwise analyze all 3 phases.
  -R, --recursive    Walk full submodule tree (sm top-down, mc bottom-up,
                     pull top-down — each phase handles its own order).
  -i, --interactive  Prompt where applicable in each phase.
  --force            Bypass per-repo my-git-submodules.ignore markers.
  -V, -VV, -D, -DD   Verbosity / debug.
  --help

Abort ([A]) in any phase stops the whole sync (exit 77). Earlier-phase
effects already committed stay — my-git's "never undo user commits" rule.
EOF
