show_help_submodules() {
  _sm_usage 2>/dev/null || cat <<EOF
my-git submodules  (aliases: sub, sm)

usage: $US sm [opts]                     (analyze; compact one-line output)
       $US sm [-T|--dry|--try]           (preview)
       $US sm go [--force]               (apply)
       $US sm --list-ignored             (audit ignored nested repos)
       $US sm --clean-leftovers          (resolve .preexisting.* leftovers)
       $US sm --sync-gitignore           (write ignored paths to .git/info/exclude)
       $US sm --emit-gitignore           (print ignore patterns to stdout)
       $US sm --unstage-embedded         (remove mis-staged embedded repos)

Merged from the standalone my-git-submodules.sh tool. Run from the top of a
super-repo to register nested .git trees as proper submodules.

Options:
  -D, --debug        set -x plus extra diagnostics
  -V, --verbose      Full multi-line block per candidate
  -i, --interactive  Prompt on failures / missing info
  -R, --recursive    Walk into every registered submodule (top-down)
  --force            With 'go', ignore per-repo ignore markers AND bypass
                     suspicious-URL guards; with embedded paths, reuse a
                     stale .git/modules/<name>/ instead of erroring out
  --help             Show extended help

────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Decision table — what 'sm go' does for each situation it encounters.
Every case that can occur is listed here; anything not in this table
is a bug. The table reflects CURRENT behavior, not aspirational design.
────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

  SITUATION                                 DEFAULT                       WITH -i
  -----------------------------------       ---------------------------   ------------------------------
  already registered (path + URL OK)        no-op                         no-op
  nested inside registered submodule        silent skip (see summary)     silent skip (see summary)
  stale registration                        auto-remove                   prompt [Y]es / [S]kip / [A]bort
   (.gitmodules entry, no dir on disk)
  carries my-git-submodules.ignore marker   silent skip (see summary)     silent skip (same)
                                            --force: treat as fresh       --force: treat as fresh
  marker + .gitmodules path mismatch        CONFLICT note, skip           prompt [U]pdate / [R]emove /
   (ignore here, registered elsewhere)                                      [S]kip / [A]bort
  moved (URL matches a different
   registered path), nested repo CLEAN      rebind in place (no re-clone) same (no extra prompt)
  moved, nested repo RISKY                  SKIP with 'push/commit        prompt [P]roceed / [S]kip /
   (uncommitted+untracked, unpushed           first' hint                   [A]bort
    branches, or stashes)
  no origin URL on nested repo              silent skip (counted          prompt for URL
                                             'missing origin URL')
  unregistered with URL, plain new          git submodule add             same
  unregistered with URL, path already
   tracked in index (embedded)              UNTRACK (git rm --cached -r)  same
                                             + git submodule add
  URL looks suspicious (unusual scheme)     add anyway (counted)          extra [U]se / [E]dit / [S]kip /
                                                                            [I]gnore perm / [A]bort first
  path matches a .gitignore pattern         silent skip (.gitignore IS    prompt [N]egate / [F]orce /
   (pre-checked with git check-ignore)       the marker — no 'add' run)     [S]kip / [A]bort
                                                                            [N] appends '!<rel>/' to the
                                                                            matching .gitignore, then adds
  stale .git/modules/<name>/                ERROR + skip                  same; --force: reuse + add
   (from an interrupted prior run)

Abort ([A]) in any -i prompt exits the whole run with code 77; under -R
it propagates up and terminates the entire recursive walk (not just the
current node).

Auto-commit (go only): at the end of a successful 'sm go', my-git
commits ONLY the paths it touched in this run — .gitmodules, registered
submodule gitlinks, removed stale paths, and any .gitignore files where
a negation was appended via [N]egate. Any unrelated changes the user
had staged or modified (other submodule gitlink drift, unrelated edits)
are LEFT ALONE — they stay staged/unstaged exactly as they were. The
commit message is like 'my-git sm: registered=2 stale-removed=1'. Under
-i, my-git prints the planned message + path list and prompts [Y]es /
[S]kip / [A]bort before committing.

Ignore marker:  writes '<nested>/.git/my-git-submodules.ignore' (delete to un-ignore).

Exclude-file sync: at the END of every 'sm go' run (even when nothing was
registered), my-git reconciles \$SUPER/.git/info/exclude so every nested
repo carrying the ignore marker is listed as /path/ there — this prevents
'git status' in the super-repo from showing those repos as Untracked. The
block is delimited by '# --- my-git-submodules: ignored nested repos ...'
markers so unrelated excludes are left alone. --sync-gitignore /
--emit-gitignore still work standalone.

Apply order (top-down): registration is applied parents-first — shortest
path first, deeper paths later. Once a parent has been registered (add or
rebind) in this run, any remaining candidate below it is skipped with a
'now nested inside registered submodule X' note; register those from
INSIDE the parent ('cd X && $US sm go'). This is the OPPOSITE of 'mc',
which is bottom-up so child commits bubble into the parent's gitlink.

Recursion (opt-in): by default 'sm' acts on ONE level — git itself only
cares about the current repo's own gitlinks; what lives inside a
registered submodule is that submodule's own concern. Pass -R /
--recursive to have 'sm' (analyze / --dry / go) walk into every
registered direct submodule top-down, mirroring what 'st' shows, so a
single '$US sm -R go' at the super-repo covers every nested .git in the
tree. [A]bort inside a nested prompt propagates: it aborts the WHOLE
walk, not just the current node. Flag-driven modes (--list-ignored /
--emit-gitignore / --clean-leftovers / --sync-gitignore /
--unstage-embedded) never recurse even with -R — they already traverse
globally from the current repo.

Stale registrations: entries in .gitmodules whose on-disk path does NOT
exist are detected and (with 'go') removed — 'git config -f .gitmodules
--remove-section submodule.<name>', plus '.git/config' cleanup,
'git rm --cached -f -- <path>', and 'rm -rf -- .git/modules/<name>'.
Under -i, each stale entry is prompted before removal. Typical cause:
a submodule was renamed on disk without updating .gitmodules, leaving
the old entry behind.

--list-ignored: lists EVERY nested repo that carries the ignore marker.
Entries that are ALSO registered in .gitmodules (same path, or same URL at
a different path) are shown with a '!! CONFLICT' remark — the marker says
"skip" but .gitmodules says "tracked", which is inconsistent state.

Ignored-path mismatch: when 'sm go' detects the conflict (ignored marker
here, but .gitmodules has this URL at a different path), it stops with
status CONFLICT and, under -i, prompts:
   [U]pdate  — rewrite .gitmodules path AND remove the marker, then rebind
               in place (no re-clone; local branches / commits / changes
               preserved).
   [R]emove  — delete the ignore marker only; the next 'sm go' will then
               see this as a normal moved submodule.
   [S]kip / [A]bort.

Rebind auto-save: when 'sm go' rebinds a moved submodule and the nested
repo has ONLY modifications to already-tracked files (no untracked files,
no unpushed commits, no stashes), sm auto-commits them with a message
listing the changed filenames and pushes to origin before re-cloning.
Anything more (untracked files, unpushed commits, stashes) still warns
and skips (or prompts with -i).

See also:      $US --help                (top-level)
EOF
}

